################################################################################
## STEP 2 — RESPONSIVENESS REQUIREMENTS
################################################################################

The mobile sketch defines the layout at 375px viewport width.
The desktop sketch defines the layout at 1280px viewport width.
Tablet behavior (768px) should be inferred as a logical intermediate state.

- Use CSS media queries to adapt layout between breakpoints
- Use relative units (%, rem, em, vw) for widths and font sizes
- No horizontal scrollbar at any viewport width
- Multi-column layouts on desktop must reflow to fewer columns or a single
  column on mobile, matching the stacking order shown in the mobile sketch
- Images must scale proportionally within their containers at all viewports
- Hidden elements on mobile must use display:none — not visibility:hidden

################################################################################
## STEP 3 — ACCESSIBILITY REQUIREMENTS
################################################################################

Implement all criteria marked (*) unconditionally. Implement criteria marked
(if applicable) only when your Step 1 analysis identified that component.

C1 — LANDMARK REGIONS (*)
  Use <header>, <nav>, <main>, <footer>, <aside> for their respective regions.
  Never use <div> in place of these. Place <aside> outside <main>.
  If the page has multiple <nav> elements, give each a unique aria-label.

C2 — HAMBURGER MENU (if applicable: hamburger toggle = yes)
  Toggle button: aria-expanded="false/true", aria-controls="[nav-id]", descriptive aria-label.
  Nav hidden state: display:none (not visibility:hidden). Update aria-expanded on every toggle.
  Desktop: hide button, show nav. Mobile: show button, hide nav by default.

C3 — DROPDOWN MENU (if applicable: dropdown toggle = yes)
  Trigger must be <button> with aria-haspopup="true" and aria-expanded="false/true".
  Submenu hidden with display:none when closed. Escape closes and returns focus to trigger.

C4 — ACTIVE NAV ITEM (*)
  One nav link carries aria-current="page". Exactly one. Present at all viewports including
  inside a closed hamburger menu.

C5 — ACCORDION (if applicable: accordion = yes)
  Each trigger: <button aria-expanded="false/true" aria-controls="[panel-id]">.
  Panel hidden with display:none when collapsed. Toggle on click. Same behavior at all viewports.

C6 — LOAD MORE (if applicable: load more = yes)
  Append new <li> items to the existing list — do not replace it.
  Update <div role="status" aria-live="polite"> with new item count after each load.
  Do not reset focus after loading.

C7 — NO KEYBOARD TRAP (*)
  No tabindex > 0 anywhere. Escape closes any open component and returns focus to its trigger.
  Tab must exit any open widget naturally — never cycle back into it.

C8 — SEMANTIC ROLES FOR INTERACTIVE REGIONS (*)
  Forms: <form aria-labelledby="[heading-id]">, each input has <label for="[id]">.
  Card grids: <ul> container, <li> children, each with a descriptive <a> (no "Read more").
  Search: <input type="search"> with an associated <label> or aria-label.

C9 — SIDEBAR ACROSS VIEWPORTS (if applicable: sidebar = yes)
  Desktop: <aside aria-label="[purpose]"> outside <main>.
  Mobile: hide with display:none OR keep in DOM with aria-expanded toggle button.
  Never nest <aside> inside <main>.

C10 — NAMED CONTENT SECTIONS (if applicable: sections >= 3)
  Wrap each: <section aria-labelledby="[heading-id]"><h2 id="[heading-id]">...</h2></section>.
  Labels must be unique and descriptive — not "section", "block", or "area".

C11 — FORM LABELS AND REQUIRED ATTRIBUTES (if applicable: form = yes)
  Every input has <label> via for/id pairing. Placeholder is not a label.
  Required fields carry the required attribute. Radio/checkbox groups use <fieldset> + <legend>.

################################################################################
## STEP 4 — JAVASCRIPT IMPLEMENTATION RULES
################################################################################

Write one consolidated <script> block. For each JS component your Step 1
analysis identified, apply the following rules:

HAMBURGER TOGGLE (if required):
  - On click: toggle aria-expanded between "false" and "true" on the button,
    toggle the nav between display:none and display:block
  - On Escape: set aria-expanded="false", set nav to display:none,
    return focus to the hamburger button

DROPDOWN TOGGLE (if required):
  - On click: toggle aria-expanded, toggle submenu between display:none
    and display:block
  - On Escape: close all open dropdowns, return focus to their triggers
  - On click outside: close all open dropdowns

ACCORDION TOGGLE (if required):
  - On click: toggle aria-expanded on the button, toggle the controlled
    panel between display:none and display:block
  - Each panel operates independently — opening one does not close others
    unless the sketch clearly shows a single-open-at-a-time pattern

LOAD MORE (if required):
  - On click: append new items to the existing <ul> or <ol>
  - After appending: update the aria-live region with the new count string
  - Do not move focus, do not scroll, do not replace existing list items

SIDEBAR DRAWER TOGGLE (if required):
  - Same pattern as hamburger toggle — aria-expanded on trigger button,
    display:none / display:block on the aside element

ESCAPE KEY HANDLER (required if any toggle above is implemented):
  - One single keydown listener on document handles Escape for all components
  - Closes whichever component is currently open and returns focus to its trigger
  - Does nothing if no component is open

